+# Wayfinding operations (local-markdown tracker)
+
+Where the map, its child tickets, blocking, and the frontier query physically
+live, for the local-markdown tracker. The default when no other tracker is
+provided.
+
+## Location
+
+A map and its tickets live under `docs/superpowers/wayfinder/<effort-slug>/`
+(gitignored — it persists across sessions but is never committed). The
+`<effort-slug>` is derived from the destination's name.
+
+- The map is `map.md`.
+- Each ticket is `tickets/NN-slug.md`, where `NN` is a zero-padded two-digit id
+  (`01`, `02`, …), assigned in creation order.
+- Per-ticket claim locks live in `.locks/`: `.locks/NN.lock` for ticket `NN`. The
+  `.locks/` dir is under the already-gitignored effort tree, so it needs no new gitignore entry.
+
+## Active-map scan
+
+A map is **active** iff it has at least one `open` ticket **or** a non-empty "Not yet
+specified" section; **finished** otherwise. The scan skips any effort dir without a `map.md`
+and surfaces only a per-map summary line — never ticket bodies:
+
+```sh
+for d in docs/superpowers/wayfinder/*/; do
+  [ -f "$d/map.md" ] || continue
+  open=$(grep -rl '^status: open' "$d/tickets" 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')
+  nys=0
+  awk '/^## Not yet specified/{f=1;next} /^## /{f=0} f' "$d/map.md" \
+    | sed 's/<!--.*-->//g' | grep -q '[^[:space:]]' && nys=1
+  if [ "$open" -gt 0 ] || [ "$nys" = 1 ]; then
+    name=$(sed -n 's/^# //p' "$d/map.md" | head -1)
+    echo "$name · open=$open"
+  fi
+done
+```
+
+`sed 's/<!--.*-->//g'` strips single-line HTML comments anywhere on a line, so a
+"Not yet specified" section holding only the template comment reads as empty (finished),
+while a real fog line survives even if it contains a `-->`. The summary line is
+`<map-name> · open=N`. Read no ticket bodies here — the scan is a
+routing aid, not the map load.
+
+## Child issue → file
+
+A ticket *is* a file in `tickets/`. Its filename `NN` is its id. Creating a
+ticket means writing the file; there is no separate tracker record.
+
+Creating a ticket also creates its empty lock so a probe never precedes it:
+`mkdir -p .locks && : > .locks/NN.lock` (relative to the effort dir; `NN` matches the
+ticket). The lock stays empty until a session claims the ticket (see [Claiming](#claiming)).
+
+## Type / claim / blocking → frontmatter
+
+Each ticket carries YAML frontmatter:
+
+```yaml
+---
+type: research        # one of: research | prototype | grilling | task (implementation tickets use the richer contract under "Implementation ticket validation")
+status: open          # one of: open | closed
+claimed-by:           # the owning session's id ($AI_SESSION_TAB), or empty = unclaimed
+blocked-by: []        # a list of ticket ids this one waits on, e.g. [01, 03]
+---
+```
+
+The ticket body is the `## Question` block, exactly as the SKILL.md ticket-body
+format defines it.
+
+## Claiming
+
+`claimed-by:` is the ticket's **durable owner** — the claiming session's id, set and
+cleared only through the compare-and-swap primitive (see [Atomic release](#atomic-release)).
+A per-ticket `flock` layered over it is the **liveness** signal: whether the owning
+session is still alive. The two are distinct — see [Two locks — do not merge](#two-locks--do-not-merge).
+
+The lock file is created empty when the ticket is (see
+[Child issue → file](#child-issue--file)), so it always exists before any probe.
+
+**To claim**, win the flock **first** — it is the mutual-exclusion gate — then CAS-set
+`claimed-by`. A detached `setsid` holder keeps the lock for the session's lifetime and
+writes its durable identity (`pid`/`sess`) only *after* acquiring the lock, so a
+losing racer never wipes the winner's content:
+
+```sh
+export LOCK=docs/superpowers/wayfinder/<effort-slug>/.locks/NN.lock
+setsid sh -c 'exec 9<>"$LOCK"; flock -n 9 || exit 7;
+              printf "pid=%s sess=%s\n" "$$" "$AI_SESSION_TAB" > "$LOCK";
+              exec sleep infinity' &
+```
+
+`$AI_SESSION_TAB` is the session identity — the one stable per-session id that crosses into
+the bwrap jail for both Claude and Codex (`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` does not). `9<>` opens
+read-write **without truncating**. Give the holder a moment, then confirm *you* hold it by
+reading the durable identity line — if it names your session you won and may CAS-set
+`claimed-by` to `$AI_SESSION_TAB`; otherwise another live session holds it, so pick a
+different ticket:
+
+```sh
+sleep 0.2
+if grep -q "sess=$AI_SESSION_TAB$" "$LOCK"; then
+  : # you hold the flock — CAS-set claimed-by to $AI_SESSION_TAB
+else
+  : # another live session owns it — do not claim; choose another ticket
+fi
+```
+
+## Frontier → scan
+
+The frontier is the set of **claimable** tickets: `status: open`, every id in
+`blocked-by:` pointing at a `closed` ticket, **and** claimable by the claim rule — either
+`claimed-by:` is empty, or it is set but the claim is **stale** (the owning session ended,
+so its flock is free). Compute it by reading frontmatter and probing each lock; there is no
+tracker UI to query.
+
+Probe a lock **read-only** — it never steals or truncates, and a missing file is free
+(belt-and-suspenders with empty-at-creation, so a freshly charted map whose tickets have no
+lock files yet never reads as held):
+
+```sh
+if [ ! -e "$LOCK" ]; then
+  echo free
+elif ( flock -n 9 || exit 1 ) 9<"$LOCK"; then
+  echo free
+else
+  echo held
+fi
+```
+
+Combine the probe with `claimed-by:`:
+
+- `claimed-by:` empty → **unclaimed**, eligible.
+- `claimed-by:` set, lock **held** → a live session owns it, **skip**.
+- `claimed-by:` set, lock **free** → **stale** (owner ended), eligible; reclaiming
+  overwrites `claimed-by:` through the CAS. Read the lock content for *who* only when held.
+
+## Resolution
+
+To resolve a ticket:
+
+1. Set `status: closed`.
+2. Write the answer into the ticket body (below the `## Question` block).
+3. Append one gist line to the map's `## Decisions so far`, linking the ticket by
+   a relative path: `- [<ticket title>](tickets/NN-slug.md) — <one-line gist>`.
+
+## Release
+
+At ticket close or phase handoff, free the holder by the **durable PID in the lock file** —
+this survives `/clear` and context compaction because the PID is read from the file, not
+from conversation memory, and is only ever applied to your own claim (same session = same
+PID namespace) — then CAS-clear or CAS-advance `claimed-by:` / `phase:`. Run this **before**
+any `/clear` the skill emits:
+
+```sh
+pid=$(sed -n 's/^pid=\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p' "$LOCK"); [ -n "$pid" ] && kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null
+```
+
+**Safety net.** `--die-with-parent` frees every holder at session end; a hard crash or
+reboot drops the kernel lock. Either way the claim becomes stale (`claimed-by:` set, lock
+free) and is auto-reclaimable (see [Repair](#repair)).
+
+## Two locks — do not merge
+
+Two distinct locks operate on a ticket; do not conflate them.
+
+- **Claim liveness lock:** the long-lived per-ticket `flock` in
+  [Claiming](#claiming). Signals whether a claimed ticket's owner session is alive; backs
+  `claimed-by:` so a claim can never strand.
+- **Edit lock:** the short `mkdir`/`O_EXCL` compare-and-swap in
+  [Atomic release](#atomic-release), held only for one file mutation, serializing
+  concurrent appends to `map.md` and every ticket write.
+
+The liveness lock does not replace the edit-lock CAS or its `claimed-by:` ownership field;
+both stay in effect for different purposes. Staleness is detected flock-free: a freed
+liveness lock marks an orphaned claim reclaimable, and no manual lease-repair is required.
+The artifact/phase repair table is unchanged.
+
+**Accepted limitation.** If the user hits `/clear` *mid-ticket* — before the ticket closes
+and with no explicit release having run — the holder survives (flock still held) and
+`claimed-by:` stays set, so the claim reads as *live*, not stale. A concurrent session
+wanting that exact ticket sees it busy and picks another (frees on teardown); the same
+session resumes its own ticket when it still recognizes the claim as its own (`claimed-by:`
+equals its session id). A mid-ticket manual `/clear` is a rare interruption, so this is
+accepted, not mitigated.
+
+## Implementation ticket validation
+
+An implementation ticket is created only after an actionable native decision
+has closed. It uses this frontmatter contract:
+
+```yaml
+---
+type: implementation
+status: open
+phase: superpowers:brainstorming
+map-path: docs/superpowers/wayfinder/example/map.md
+origin-ticket-id: 12
+origin-ticket: docs/superpowers/wayfinder/example/tickets/12-auth-flow.md
+spec:
+plan:
+claimed-by:
+blocked-by: []
+revision: 1
+---
+```
+
+First resolve the physical repository root with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`,
+then `realpath -e`. Resolve every non-empty ticket, map, origin, artifact,
+blocker, and handoff path with `realpath -e`; it must be an in-root regular file
+and its stored value must equal the canonical root-relative path. Reject absolute,
+lexically escaping, symlink-escaping, missing, directory, and noncanonical values.
+The valid states are:
+
+| Status | Phase | spec | plan |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| open | superpowers:brainstorming | empty | empty |
+| open | superpowers:writing-plans | valid file | empty |
+| open | superpowers:executing-plans | valid file | valid file |
+| closed | implemented | valid file | valid file |
+
+Reject every other implementation-state/path combination. A closed or
+implemented ticket is terminal and is never selected.
+
+## Creation lock
+
+Create an implementation ticket under a `mkdir` (or `O_EXCL`) creation lock keyed
+by the canonical physical `origin-ticket` path plus `origin-ticket-id`. While
+holding it, scan by that pair: reuse exactly one existing ticket, fail without a
+replacement for duplicate pairs, or create one new ticket at revision 1. Same IDs
+with different canonical origins are distinct. Write the canonical origin
+resolution before unlock; every acquired lock is released on success and failure,
+and cleanup failure is a protocol failure.
+
+## Selection and claims
+
+The selector validates a ticket, accepts an empty `claimed-by` **or a stale claim**
+(`claimed-by` set but its liveness lock probes free per [Frontier → scan](#frontier--scan)),
+and rejects a **live** claim (`claimed-by` set, lock held) and any active blocker. It first chooses eligible
+`superpowers:executing-plans`, then eligible `superpowers:writing-plans`, sorting
+each phase by ascending numeric ticket id, then records a reasoned choice between
+eligible native decision work and eligible implementation
+`superpowers:brainstorming`. Wayfinder claims the selected ticket through the
+mutation primitive below before handoff; reclaiming a stale claim overwrites its
+non-empty `claimed-by` through that same primitive. Native tickets remain eligible under the
+existing frontier rules.
+
+## Atomic release
+
+Every claim, release, blocker update, and repair calls one mandatory ordered
+ticket-level compare-and-swap primitive. Normalize the ticket to its physical
+path; acquire its scoped `mkdir`/`O_EXCL` lock; reread and revalidate expected
+revision, status, phase, owner, blockers, artifacts, and canonical paths under
+that lock; preserve its mode into a same-directory temporary file; write the
+authorized update; atomically rename it over the ticket; remove the temporary file
+on every pre-rename failure; then unlock. Cleanup failure is a protocol failure.
+Each lifecycle mutation and repair increments revision exactly once. A successful
+normal transition compares expected status, phase, revision, and owner, writes its
+authorized fields, clears `claimed-by`, and increments revision once.
+An initial guarded claim may change an empty `claimed-by:`, **or a stale non-empty
+`claimed-by:` whose liveness lock probes free** (see [Frontier → scan](#frontier--scan)),
+to its expected owner; no normal transition combines with repair.
+
+The downstream owner releases the same ticket only after it has persisted its
+artifact and phase.
+A declined approval writes nothing, retaining the ticket, claim, and current phase so the
+live session keeps iterating; a failed or interrupted stage writes nothing and leaves the
+claim — when that session ends its liveness lock frees, making the claim stale and
+auto-reclaimable (see [Repair](#repair)).
+
+A normal release clears the caller's own `claimed-by` back to empty while preserving
+`origin-ticket-id`. A **stale** claim is reclaimed by the guarded claim above (see [Repair](#repair)).
+
+## Repair
+
+On an implementation ticket, `blocked-by` is a YAML sequence of canonical
+native-ticket paths — unlike a native map ticket's `blocked-by`, which lists bare
+ticket ids. Missing,
+malformed, missing-target, non-native, unparseable-ID, and open entries are active
+blockers. Add through the primitive; remove one only through a guarded mutation
+after its referenced native ticket closes.
+
+Use the mutation primitive for same-ticket block/resume, artifact rollback,
+contradiction repair, corrupt closed-state repair, and the final terminal
+closed/implemented transition. A **stale** owner (its liveness lock probes free) is reclaimed directly by the guarded claim
+in [Claiming](#claiming) / [Atomic release](#atomic-release), which overwrites the non-empty
+`claimed-by` under the lock — the freed flock proves the owning session ended, so orphaned
+claims need no manual lease-repair.
+Never create a successor ticket while repairing or resuming.
+
+The repair table is deterministic and is not combined with a normal transition:
+
+| Invalid state | Repair |
+| --- | --- |
+| `spec` is invalid in `superpowers:writing-plans` | Clear only `spec`; return to `superpowers:brainstorming`. |
+| `plan` is invalid in `superpowers:executing-plans` | Clear only `plan`; return to `superpowers:writing-plans`. |
+| Phase/artifact contradiction | Clear the newest invalid artifact; return to the immediately preceding valid phase. |
+| Invalid `closed`/`implemented` | Clear only invalid artifacts; return to the latest valid open phase. |
+
+Preserve every valid artifact, all unknown frontmatter and body content, and
+verification evidence. Never reopen a valid terminal ticket.
+
+Before every transition to closed/implemented, record a `## Verification evidence`
+section with commands run, exit status, date, and a concise output summary.
+A terminal transition that lacks this evidence is invalid; it remains open and is
+returned to the same ticket's recovery flow.
